git clone <repo URL> → used to download an existing remote repository.

git fetch → downloads remote changes without modifying my current branch.

git merge <branch> →  It simply takes the history and code from one local branch (like feature/day5-git) and blends it directly into your current local branch (like main)

git pull → fetches remote changes and then integrates those changes into my current branch
basically, git fetch + git merge.

git switch -c feature/day5-git → create a new branch and immediately switch to it.

git push -u origin feature/day5-git → uploads the local branch to the remote repo and links the branch to you main/origin branch

git switch main → let us moves out of current branch and switches the active working environment back to the main branch.

good practice to run "git pull" after every "git switch main" to integrate any changes made by others to the remote repo.

git log --oneline --graph --all - displays the branch history--in graphical way.


