GIT revert vs reset

git revert <Commit ID> →  git revert creates a new commit that reverses a previous commit.

Use git revert for shared public branches (like main on GitHub)

git reset → It moves your active branch pointer back to a previous commit, completing erasing the newer commit.

git reset --soft <commit-id> : Rewinds history but keeps your code changes sitting in your staging area. Best for re-doing a commit message.

git reset --mixed <commit-id> (Default): Rewinds history, keeps your code changes, but un-stages them.

git reset --hard <commit-id> : Rewinds history and destroys all code changes made after that point. Your project folder resets completely.

